/*
 * SPDX-FileCopyrightText: no
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: CC0-1.0
 *
 * Stylesheet of the Companion OS installer branding. Colours are CTRL_* placeholders between at-signs that stage-installer.sh fills with the
 * brand tokens (vendor/brand/tokens/tokens.sh, see the table in that script): the brand canon is the only place a hex value is typed
 * (the style-canon lint reads this tree).
 *
 * Why there is more than a font size here. The installer takes its colours from the Plasma colour scheme of the live session,
 * CI-OS-Dark, and that scheme (backends/_mixins/kde-plasma/CI-OS-Dark.colors) paints Window, View, Button AND Selection with the same
 * brand teal (10,99,88). Everything Qt draws with the palette's Highlight is therefore invisible, which is what the ISO #4 frames showed:
 *   - the progress bar of the installing page: its filled part had the window colour, only the unfilled track was lighter (inverted);
 *   - the selected row of the keyboard layout / variant lists, the bootloader list, the package tree and the partition table;
 *   - the Welcome page's help icon, a stock dark pictogram from Calamares' own resources (:/data/images/help.svg), unreadable on teal;
 *   - (ISO #5) the popup of every combo box, the zone list of the location page: no highlight for the current entry or the one under the
 *     pointer; and the checked radio buttons of the partition page: a bare white dot without its ring.
 * The scheme is shared with the Ubuntu edition and not ours to change, so the installer sets its own colours here, for the widgets it
 * actually uses. Verified against the pinned installer (3.4.2-13, the package the launcher keeps): the object names #supportButton,
 * #donateButton, #releaseNotesButton and #knownIssuesButton are the ones its own stock stylesheet uses and are in the strings of its
 * welcome module; the rest are Qt classes (QProgressBar, QAbstractItemView, QComboBox, QRadioButton), which Calamares documents as
 * styleable. The result was rendered offscreen (Qt 6.12, the Breeze style, the CI-OS-Dark palette and this sheet; PySide6 in a scratch
 * container: not Calamares itself); the installer tests of this edition (installer-config.py) hold the contrast numbers.
 *
 * Contrast (WCAG 2.1, checked by iso/tests/installer-config.py from the resolved tokens, not by eye):
 *   selected row     dark text on sage          13.98:1 (AAA), the row against the window 5.43:1 (needs 3:1 as a graphic)
 *   progress text    light text on the fill      4.58:1 (AA, needs 4.5), on the track 16.16:1
 *   progress fill    against the track           3.53:1 (needs 3:1), the track's outline against the window 3.52:1
 *   popup entry      dark text on sage           13.98:1 (AAA), the row against the window 5.43:1
 *   radio button     ring against the window     3.52:1 (muted), checked ring and dot 5.43:1 (sage); needs 3:1 as a graphic
 */

QWidget {
    font: 13px;
}

QDialogButtonBox {
    dialogbuttonbox-buttons-have-icons: 0;
}

/*** Installing page: the progress bar.
 *
 * A dark track with a light outline and a brand-teal fill, the percentage centred in it. The text is light on both (the fill is the
 * brightest brand teal on which light text still reads: 4.58:1); a light fill would need dark text, which would not read on the track.
 */
QProgressBar {
    background-color: @CTRL_TRACK@;
    border: 1px solid @CTRL_TRACK_OUTLINE@;
    border-radius: 4px;
    color: @CTRL_FILL_TEXT@;
    text-align: center;
    min-height: 18px;
    max-height: 18px;
}

QProgressBar::chunk {
    background-color: @CTRL_FILL@;
    border-radius: 3px;
}

/*** Selected rows of every list, tree and table (keyboard page, bootloader chooser, package groups, partitions, combo box popups).
 *
 * selection-background-color / selection-color set the palette's Highlight / HighlightedText for the view, which is what Breeze paints
 * selected and hovered rows with. The sidebar of the main window has its own delegate and its own colours (branding.desc style:), so
 * it is not touched.
 */
QAbstractItemView {
    selection-background-color: @CTRL_SELECTION@;
    selection-color: @CTRL_SELECTION_TEXT@;
}

/*** Combo box popups (the Region and Zone lists of the location page, the keyboard model, the storage device and file system lists).
 *
 * ISO #5's zone list (the current entry New York, the pointer on any entry) showed no highlight at all. The list-style popup that Breeze
 * uses is painted by a plain item delegate, which ignores every item rule of a stylesheet and draws the current and the hovered entry with
 * the style's own focus decoration, a translucent wash that is invisible on this window colour (checked offscreen with Qt 6.12 and
 * Breeze: `QAbstractItemView { selection-... }` above and `QComboBox QAbstractItemView::item:selected` change nothing there). The
 * menu-style popup (`combobox-popup: 1`, the style hint Qt exposes to stylesheets) is painted by the stylesheet itself: the entry under the
 * pointer or the keyboard gets the same sage row with dark text (13.98:1) as every other list, and the current one a check mark. The
 * transparent base rule matters: without a rule of its own for every entry, the rows the sheet does not style keep Breeze's native empty
 * check boxes. The popup scrolls with arrows and the mouse wheel instead of a scroll bar.
 */
QComboBox {
    combobox-popup: 1;
}

QComboBox::item {
    background-color: transparent;
}

QComboBox::item:selected {
    background-color: @CTRL_SELECTION@;
    color: @CTRL_SELECTION_TEXT@;
}

/*** Radio buttons (the choices of the partition page: Erase disk, Manual partitioning, ...).
 *
 * Breeze draws the checked state as a dot in the palette's Highlight and the ring in the Button colour, and on this scheme both are the
 * window colour: ISO #5 showed the selected choice as a bare white dot with no ring. The indicator is drawn here instead: a ring in the
 * muted outline colour (3.52:1 on the window), and when checked a sage ring with a sage dot and a gap of window colour between them
 * (5.43:1 on the window, a graphic needs 3:1). 12 px plus two 2 px borders is a 16 px circle: border-radius is half of that.
 */
QRadioButton::indicator {
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    border: 2px solid @CTRL_TRACK_OUTLINE@;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background-color: transparent;
}

QRadioButton::indicator:hover {
    border-color: @CTRL_SELECTION@;
}

QRadioButton::indicator:checked {
    border: 2px solid @CTRL_SELECTION@;
    background-color: qradialgradient(cx: 0.5, cy: 0.5, radius: 0.5, fx: 0.5, fy: 0.5, stop: 0 @CTRL_SELECTION@, stop: 0.5 @CTRL_SELECTION@, stop: 0.62 transparent, stop: 1 transparent);
}

/*** Welcome page: the four link buttons.
 *
 * Calamares puts a stock pictogram (dark on transparent) on each of them; on the teal window it is a dark smudge next to the text. The
 * links keep their label and lose the icon. qproperty-iconSize as well as icon-size, because the first sets the widget's own property
 * and wins even if the code of a later Calamares sets an icon size itself.
 */
#supportButton,
#donateButton,
#releaseNotesButton,
#knownIssuesButton {
    icon-size: 0px;
    qproperty-iconSize: 0px 0px;
}
